][1072,463]" drawing-order="4" hint="" /><node index="4" text="" resource-id="" class="android.view.View" package="br.gov.sp.pcsp.launcher" content-desc="" checkable="false" checked="false" clickable="false" enabled="true" focusable="false" focused="false" scrollable="false" long-clickable="false" password="false" selected="false" bounds="[1072,408][1212,411]" drawing-order="5" hint="" /></node><node index="2" text="Texto original" resource-id="" class="android.widget.TextView" package="br.gov.sp.pcsp.launcher" content-desc="" checkable="false" checked="false" clickable="false" enabled="true" focusable="false" focused="false" scrollable="false" long-clickable="false" password="false" selected="false" bounds="[60,493][1212,532]" drawing-order="3" hint="" /><node index="3" text="The city council approved on Tuesday a comprehensive plan to modernize the municipal water network over the next five years. According to the proposal, aging pipes in the central district will be replaced first, since recent inspections found leaks responsible for nearly a third of the city's daily water loss. Engineers estimate that the project will reduce consumption by about eighteen percent once the first phase is complete. Local businesses welcomed the announcement, although some owners asked for clearer schedules to avoid disruptions during the busiest months of the year. The council promised weekly updates and a public dashboard showing the progress of each street. Environmental groups, in turn, requested that the plan include stricter controls on industrial discharge, arguing that pipe replacement alone will not protect the rivers that cross the region. The mayor said the investment will be funded through a combination of municipal savings and a long-term loan, and insisted that household bills will not increase before the second phase. A public hearing is scheduled for next month, when residents will be able to present suggestions and formally question the technical team " resource-id